1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,804
Each runner here is driven by a different decision model.

2
00:00:03,804 --> 00:00:07,493
Here's exactly what each model is asked, what it can answer,

3
00:00:07,493 --> 00:00:09,030
and why the race is fair.

4
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:11,610
First, what does a decision model do?

5
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:13,263
It doesn't write text.

6
00:00:13,263 --> 00:00:18,796
It gets a described situation and described options, and answers with one choice,

7
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:20,640
and a probability for each.

8
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:26,945
Here, every model chooses from the same five moves: left, right, jump, roll,

9
00:00:26,945 --> 00:00:28,160
and keep going.

10
00:00:28,310 --> 00:00:29,800
And there are no turns.

11
00:00:29,950 --> 00:00:32,554
Each runner keeps one question in flight.

12
00:00:32,554 --> 00:00:36,810
The moment the answer arrives, the move is made, and it asks again.

13
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,966
The world keeps moving while a model thinks.

14
00:00:39,966 --> 00:00:45,567
Laya answers in about a quarter of a second, making about four decisions a second.

15
00:00:45,567 --> 00:00:49,529
OpenAI takes about half a second, and makes just over one.

16
00:00:49,529 --> 00:00:51,510
Speed is part of the contest.

17
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:53,210
So where do the questions go?

18
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:56,412
The game runs in your browser and keeps the clock.

19
00:00:56,412 --> 00:01:00,760
Over one WebSocket, it talks to a small Python server on your computer.

20
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:06,097
The server turns what each runner sees into text, and asks its model:

21
00:01:06,097 --> 00:01:12,111
OpenAI's GPT-6 Luna, Jev, Perplexity or Cloudflare's Clef in the cloud, or Laya,

22
00:01:12,111 --> 00:01:13,840
right on your computer.

23
00:01:13,990 --> 00:01:18,110
The API keys stay in that server, and never reach the page.

24
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:19,634
Now, the heart of it.

25
00:01:19,634 --> 00:01:23,625
Here's a real question, sent to Perplexity in the right lane,

26
00:01:23,625 --> 00:01:25,850
at the start of a five-model race.

27
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,580
First, its runner: right lane, running, 15 meters a second.

28
00:01:30,730 --> 00:01:35,860
Its answers take about half a second, so its move lands about 8 meters further on.

29
00:01:36,010 --> 00:01:41,077
Then the lanes, its own first: a low barrier 12 meters ahead,

30
00:01:41,077 --> 00:01:46,310
reached in 0.79 seconds, 0.23 seconds after its answer arrives.

31
00:01:46,470 --> 00:01:50,064
Then the other two lanes, each up to 100 meters ahead,

32
00:01:50,064 --> 00:01:52,260
with up to four obstacles a lane.

33
00:01:52,410 --> 00:01:53,886
Trains get special care.

34
00:01:53,886 --> 00:01:58,068
A ramp is told together with the train it leads onto, like this one,

35
00:01:58,068 --> 00:02:00,036
up onto a standing train's roof.

36
00:02:00,036 --> 00:02:05,079
And a train right beside a runner says that moving into that lane now would bounce

37
00:02:05,079 --> 00:02:05,510
it off.

38
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:10,755
Then its place, third of five, and every other runner: its lane, its gap,

39
00:02:10,755 --> 00:02:12,290
and whether it's down.

40
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:16,230
And last, the ground breaking away, 40 meters behind.

41
00:02:16,390 --> 00:02:20,217
The instructions are the same for everyone, with the game's timings,

42
00:02:20,217 --> 00:02:21,680
measured from its physics.

43
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:27,567
A jump clears a low barrier reached 0.1 to 0.55 seconds after jumping.

44
00:02:27,567 --> 00:02:31,590
A roll gets under a high one within 0.64 seconds.

45
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:35,580
It gives facts and rules, never which move to make.

46
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:41,601
If Perplexity answers in its usual time, it jumps 0.23 seconds before the barrier:

47
00:02:41,601 --> 00:02:42,890
inside the window.

48
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:49,401
This time it answered jump, 99% sure, in 0.66 seconds, a little slower than usual.

49
00:02:49,401 --> 00:02:54,830
So it jumped 0.13 seconds before the barrier, still inside the window,

50
00:02:54,830 --> 00:02:56,382
near its early edge.

51
00:02:56,382 --> 00:03:00,260
That's why each model is told its own answer time.

52
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:06,315
All of it goes out as a short JSON request: the System One shape for Jev, Perplexity,

53
00:03:06,315 --> 00:03:09,302
Clef and Laya, or OpenAI's Decisions shape.

54
00:03:09,302 --> 00:03:10,830
Same words either way.

55
00:03:10,970 --> 00:03:14,511
The answer is one move, with a probability for each.

56
00:03:14,511 --> 00:03:19,890
Press D to watch them live, with each model's latency and decisions per second,

57
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:22,410
and its record over the races so far.

58
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:23,990
So, is it fair?

59
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:27,880
Every runner gets the same track, and every race a new one.

60
00:03:28,030 --> 00:03:32,248
Start slots rotate one place each race, so over five races,

61
00:03:32,248 --> 00:03:35,680
each of five models starts once from every slot.

62
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:39,065
Every model gets the same code and the same words.

63
00:03:39,065 --> 00:03:41,985
Only its own position and answer time differ.

64
00:03:41,985 --> 00:03:44,970
And the five moves work the same for everyone.

65
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:48,606
Before a race, every model must answer one question.

66
00:03:48,606 --> 00:03:52,360
If one can't, nothing starts, and you're told which one.

67
00:03:52,510 --> 00:03:53,437
Want to check?

68
00:03:53,437 --> 00:03:57,809
Three files show all of it, and the tests run on recorded answers,

69
00:03:57,809 --> 00:03:59,199
without a single key.

70
00:03:59,199 --> 00:04:01,650
Then pick your models, and race them.
